Wiring:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.
- Joystick SW -> Arduino Uno digital pin 2.

VRx and VRy are analog outputs from the joystick module to the Uno. With the module powered from 5 V, each axis normally produces a voltage between about 0 V and 5 V, which is within the Uno's analog-input range when using the default ADC reference. The center position is typically near mid-supply, so the ADC reading is usually near 512, but real modules can be offset.

The SW pin is normally a mechanical switch signal. On common joystick modules, pressing the stick connects SW to GND. Because no external resistor is allowed, configure pin 2 as INPUT_PULLUP. That enables the Uno's internal pull-up resistor, so:
- not pressed -> HIGH
- pressed -> LOW

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;
const int SW_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(SW_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(SW_PIN) == LOW);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.print(y);
  Serial.print(" Button=");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "RELEASED");

  delay(100);
}
```

Do not configure A0 or A1 as digital outputs. They are being used as ADC inputs, while SW is a separate digital input. No external pull-up resistor is required for SW because INPUT_PULLUP provides the needed bias internally.

The joystick's VRx and VRy pins are analog voltage outputs, so they must be connected to ADC-capable analog inputs on the Arduino Uno. Digital pins 3 and 4 can read only digital HIGH/LOW states with digitalRead(); they do not measure the joystick's continuously varying axis voltages.

Correct wiring:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.
- Joystick SW -> Arduino Uno digital pin 2.

The two axis outputs normally vary from near 0 V to near 5 V as the stick moves. With the Uno's default ADC reference, analogRead(A0) and analogRead(A1) return values from 0 to 1023. The center is often around 512, but real modules can be offset, so do not assume the exact center value without measuring it.

On common joystick modules, pressing the stick connects SW to GND. Configure pin 2 as INPUT_PULLUP so no external resistor is required. The logic is therefore active-low: released = HIGH and pressed = LOW.

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;
const int SW_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(SW_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(SW_PIN) == LOW);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.print(y);
  Serial.print(" Button=");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "RELEASED");

  delay(100);
}
```

Do not try to recover analog axis position by calling digitalRead() on VRx or VRy. That would collapse each continuous axis voltage into only HIGH or LOW and lose the position information.

The wiring can stay unchanged:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> A0.
- Joystick VRy -> A1.
- Joystick SW -> digital pin 2.

The error is in the switch logic, not the wiring. On common joystick modules, pressing the stick connects SW to GND. With pin 2 configured as INPUT_PULLUP, the Uno's internal pull-up resistor holds the input HIGH while the switch is open. Pressing the switch then pulls the input LOW.

Therefore:
- HIGH = released
- LOW = pressed

The corrected sketch is:

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;
const int SW_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(SW_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(SW_PIN) == LOW);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.print(y);
  Serial.print(" Button=");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "RELEASED");

  delay(100);
}
```

Do not change INPUT_PULLUP to INPUT unless an external bias resistor is added, because the user's constraints explicitly forbid extra resistors. With plain INPUT and no external pull-up or pull-down, the switch input can float when released and produce unstable readings.

The wiring is appropriate for a common 5 V analog joystick module:
- VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- VRx -> A0.
- VRy -> A1.

VRx and VRy are analog voltage outputs. The Uno ADC converts each input into a value from 0 to 1023. A nominal center is often near half scale, but the mechanical potentiometers, supply tolerance and ADC noise mean the resting values are rarely exactly 512. Therefore a test such as x == 512 && y == 512 is too strict.

Use a dead zone that covers the measured idle spread. For example, with the observed readings, a center band of about 490 to 525 gives some margin without requiring extra hardware. Values below the band indicate one direction and values above the band indicate the opposite direction.

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;

const int LOW_CENTER = 490;
const int HIGH_CENTER = 525;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.print(y);
  Serial.print(" State=");

  bool xCentered = (x >= LOW_CENTER && x <= HIGH_CENTER);
  bool yCentered = (y >= LOW_CENTER && y <= HIGH_CENTER);

  if (xCentered && yCentered) {
    Serial.println("CENTER");
  } else if (x < LOW_CENTER) {
    Serial.println("LEFT");
  } else if (x > HIGH_CENTER) {
    Serial.println("RIGHT");
  } else if (y < LOW_CENTER) {
    Serial.println("DOWN");
  } else {
    Serial.println("UP");
  }

  delay(100);
}
```

The exact mapping of increasing or decreasing X/Y values to LEFT/RIGHT/UP/DOWN can be reversed by the physical orientation of the joystick module. If movement labels are opposite on the actual hardware, swap the corresponding labels in software rather than changing the electrical wiring. For a more precise build, measure the resting range of the actual joystick and choose the dead-zone limits from those measurements.

The wiring can stay unchanged:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.

VRx and VRy are analog voltage outputs from the joystick module. A0 and A1 must therefore be used as high-impedance inputs while measuring them.

Configuring A0 as OUTPUT and writing it HIGH changes the electrical role of the pin. Instead of only measuring the voltage produced by VRx, the Uno actively drives A0 toward 5 V. The joystick potentiometer is also trying to set that same node to a position-dependent voltage. Those two sources can oppose each other, causing incorrect readings and unnecessary current through the joystick's potentiometer network. Software should not actively drive a node that is being used as an analog output by the sensor.

Remove the OUTPUT configuration and any digitalWrite() on A0 or A1. analogRead() can use the Uno analog pins directly as inputs.

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.println(y);

  delay(100);
}
```

There is no need to call pinMode(A0, INPUT) or pinMode(A1, INPUT) for normal analogRead() use, although leaving the pins as inputs is essential. Also do not enable INPUT_PULLUP on VRx or VRy, because the internal pull-up would bias the analog voltage and distort the position reading.

If the X axis still remains stuck after removing the output drive, power the circuit off and inspect the wiring and joystick module before continuing; the earlier contention may have stressed the module or pin.

The wiring is electrically reasonable:

- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 3.3V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.

VRx and VRy are analog voltage outputs referenced to the joystick supply. With the module powered from 3.3 V, the axis voltages normally vary from near 0 V to near 3.3 V rather than all the way to 5 V.

By default, the Uno ADC uses approximately the board's 5 V supply as its analog reference. Therefore an input near 3.3 V does not produce an ADC code near 1023. The expected full-scale proportion is approximately 3.3/5.0 of the ADC range, so a top reading in the high 600s is plausible. It is not evidence by itself that the joystick is faulty.

Do not hard-code 675 as a universal maximum. The Uno supply, its 3.3 V rail, ADC reference, joystick potentiometers and mechanical travel all have tolerances. Measure the actual minimum and maximum values reached by the specific joystick and use those measured endpoints for application scaling.

A simple sketch can keep measured calibration limits as constants:

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;

// Replace these with values measured from the actual joystick.
const int X_MIN = 5;
const int X_MAX = 675;
const int Y_MIN = 7;
const int Y_MAX = 672;

int toPercent(int value, int low, int high) {
  value = constrain(value, low, high);
  return map(value, low, high, 0, 100);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  int xRaw = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int yRaw = analogRead(Y_PIN);

  int xPercent = toPercent(xRaw, X_MIN, X_MAX);
  int yPercent = toPercent(yRaw, Y_MIN, Y_MAX);

  Serial.print("X raw=");
  Serial.print(xRaw);
  Serial.print(" X%=");
  Serial.print(xPercent);

  Serial.print(" Y raw=");
  Serial.print(yRaw);
  Serial.print(" Y%=");
  Serial.println(yPercent);

  delay(100);
}
```

The percentage here represents position across the calibrated electrical travel of this joystick, not percentage of the Uno's entire 0-to-5 V ADC range. Keeping the normal 5 V ADC reference also means the joystick's 3.3 V output is safely below the Uno analog-input supply range.

If the goal were instead to make 3.3 V correspond more closely to ADC code 1023, the ADC reference strategy would have to change. That is not required for accurate joystick position measurement, and this answer keeps the user's stated wiring and 3.3 V supply constraint unchanged.

Disconnect power immediately. The joystick supply polarity is reversed: its VCC pin is tied to GND and its GND pin is tied to +5 V. That is an electrical fault, not a software problem. Reverse polarity can force current through parts of the joystick module in unintended directions, produce invalid axis voltages, cause heating, and permanently damage the module.

With all power removed, correct the wiring to:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.
- Joystick SW -> Arduino Uno digital pin 2.

VRx and VRy are analog outputs from the joystick to the Uno. They must be measured by A0 and A1; the Uno must not drive those nodes. The SW line is a separate digital switch input. For a common joystick module whose switch closes to GND when pressed, pin 2 can use INPUT_PULLUP so released reads HIGH and pressed reads LOW.

After rewiring, inspect the board for visible damage or odor before restoring power. Power it only briefly at first and check whether the module remains cool. If it becomes warm again, disconnect power immediately and do not keep testing it in software; the earlier reverse-polarity connection may already have damaged the module.

Only after the hardware remains electrically normal should you test readings with code such as:

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;
const int SW_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(SW_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(SW_PIN) == LOW);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.print(y);
  Serial.print(" Button=");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "RELEASED");

  delay(100);
}
```

Do not try to compensate for reverse power polarity with calibration, different thresholds, or alternate code. The supply wiring must be physically correct first.

The physical wiring can remain unchanged:
- Joystick VCC -> Arduino Uno 5V.
- Joystick GND -> Arduino Uno GND.
- Joystick VRx -> Arduino Uno A0.
- Joystick VRy -> Arduino Uno A1.

The saturation is caused by the ADC reference selection, not by the joystick wiring. On an Arduino Uno, analogReference(INTERNAL) selects the internal reference of about 1.1 V for ADC conversion. A joystick powered from 5 V can produce axis voltages from near 0 V to near 5 V. Any axis voltage at or above the selected ADC reference will convert at or near the maximum code, so much of the joystick travel can appear as 1023.

The 5 V-range joystick outputs are not inherently unsafe for the Uno analog inputs because the Uno itself is operating from 5 V and the axis signals remain within the board supply range. The problem is measurement range: a roughly 0-to-5 V signal is being compared with a roughly 1.1 V ADC reference.

Use the default ADC reference for this wiring so the ADC range matches the 5 V-powered joystick:

```cpp
const int X_PIN = A0;
const int Y_PIN = A1;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  analogReference(DEFAULT);
}

void loop() {
  int x = analogRead(X_PIN);
  int y = analogRead(Y_PIN);

  Serial.print("X=");
  Serial.print(x);
  Serial.print(" Y=");
  Serial.println(y);

  delay(100);
}
```

With the default reference, an axis voltage near 0 V maps near 0 and a voltage near the Uno supply/reference maps near 1023. The center will usually be near mid-scale but may not be exactly 512 because of joystick and supply tolerances.

Changing the ADC reference does not change the electrical voltage produced by VRx or VRy; it only changes the voltage against which the ADC measures those inputs. Therefore software cannot make a 0-to-5 V joystick signal fit a 1.1 V conversion range without saturation. Under the stated no-extra-components constraint, DEFAULT is the appropriate reference for this 5 V-powered joystick wiring.
